1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:03,000
Weapons.

2
00:00:03,001 --> 00:00:07,000
From handguns to atomic bombs.

3
00:00:07,001 --> 00:00:12,000
Each designed to protect and kill.

4
00:00:12,001 --> 00:00:17,000
How do we learn to direct and control their deadly force?

5
00:00:17,001 --> 00:00:22,000
Whether it's by traditional methods or through high-tech simulators.

6
00:00:22,001 --> 00:00:29,000
The training ground is a place called a firing range.

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:33,000
Now on Modern Marvels.

8
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:04,740
They respond to a call and don't know who or what is waiting for them when they arrive.

9
00:01:05,660 --> 00:01:08,280
Years of training will be tested in a split second.

10
00:01:08,880 --> 00:01:10,220
Stop! Stop!

11
00:01:12,400 --> 00:01:15,620
The armed suspect is gunned down in a blaze of bullets.

12
00:01:16,180 --> 00:01:21,460
Only this time, there's no blood, no body bags, no chalked outline of a corpse.

13
00:01:22,460 --> 00:01:25,460
This is a modern-day high-tech firing range,

14
00:01:25,461 --> 00:01:30,440
a simulator that combines all of the lights, camera, and action of a Hollywood movie.

15
00:01:31,160 --> 00:01:35,040
For the law enforcement community, the most important aspect of it is the realism,

16
00:01:35,600 --> 00:01:41,580
the interactivity, and the ability to create scenarios that they may face in their day-to-day job.

17
00:01:41,581 --> 00:01:44,220
Is there anything better to do than stop us on a Sunday morning?

18
00:01:44,400 --> 00:01:47,180
Just need to see the ladies' driver's license. Just relax.

19
00:01:47,960 --> 00:01:52,380
Using actors, real-life scenes are videotaped, transferred to CDs,

20
00:01:52,381 --> 00:01:55,220
and then projected on a large video screen.

21
00:01:56,540 --> 00:01:59,080
Computer hardware and software control the scenarios

22
00:01:59,081 --> 00:02:03,300
so they can be programmed for the suspect to respond in many different ways.

23
00:02:04,580 --> 00:02:06,980
They can lay down their weapon or fire back.

24
00:02:07,080 --> 00:02:07,920
Hold it! Stop!

25
00:02:12,800 --> 00:02:16,620
Officers use training weapons that have been modified to fire laser beams.

26
00:02:16,900 --> 00:02:20,320
They look, feel, even sound like the real thing.

27
00:02:22,380 --> 00:02:28,000
We use a crack and thump sound methodology where the trainee can actually hear the crack

28
00:02:28,001 --> 00:02:33,680
of the round and the thump that he would hear as the sound waves travel to him from the

29
00:02:33,681 --> 00:02:36,140
distance of where the target is simulated.

30
00:02:36,860 --> 00:02:42,300
One of the leaders in simulator firing ranges is a Georgia-based company known as FATS,

31
00:02:42,520 --> 00:02:44,720
which stands for Firearms Training Systems.

32
00:02:45,820 --> 00:02:51,280
There's something over 4,000 FATS simulators in use today in 52 countries.

33
00:02:52,380 --> 00:02:57,820
And along with those simulators are approximately 40,000 simulation weapons.

34
00:02:59,380 --> 00:03:02,960
But not all simulators use laser weapons and video screens.

35
00:03:03,460 --> 00:03:09,140
Some simulators use paper movie screens, making it possible for shooters to use real bullets.

36
00:03:11,140 --> 00:03:15,000
Some of the special forces units may be more inclined to use live fire.

37
00:03:15,001 --> 00:03:20,320
With projected image targets, the targets that can be used are really only limited by imagination.

38
00:03:22,380 --> 00:03:25,860
That's a concept that would stagger the imagination of frontiersmen,

39
00:03:26,180 --> 00:03:30,160
who used to take target practice by shooting at a mark on a tree.

40
00:03:32,680 --> 00:03:38,040
Using a tree as a target was more humane than some of the target practice that took place in ancient times.

41
00:03:40,080 --> 00:03:43,620
Homer's Iliad refers to Greeks refining their skills with a bow and arrow

42
00:03:43,621 --> 00:03:47,940
by practicing shooting real pigeons resting on tall poles.

43
00:03:47,941 --> 00:03:54,940
14th century Austria was the scene of one of the most legendary displays of marksmanship

44
00:03:55,340 --> 00:03:56,968
and target practice.

45
00:03:56,969 --> 00:04:02,340
It involved an apple, an arrow, a little boy and his father, whose

46
00:04:03,140 --> 00:04:04,558
name was William Tell.

47
00:04:04,559 --> 00:04:08,940
Tell was ordered to shoot an apple from his son's head by a ruthless

48
00:04:08,941 --> 00:04:13,940
local ruler named Gessler as punishment for Tell not kneeling at the sight of the ruler's

49
00:04:13,941 --> 00:04:22,720
Tell succeeded, but that wasn't the last time Gessler witnessed Tell's amazing marksmanship.

50
00:04:24,340 --> 00:04:27,580
Tell later slew Gessler with a single arrow through the heart.

51
00:04:31,240 --> 00:04:37,360
Fortunately, by the 16th century, target practice had become much more civilized and gave rise

52
00:04:37,361 --> 00:04:39,180
to sports shooting clubs in Germany.

53
00:04:40,180 --> 00:04:45,640
Members participated in one-shot matches and fired at elaborately painted wooden targets.

54
00:04:47,700 --> 00:04:54,700
As rifles and pistols gained popularity in Europe and America, most firing ranges were simply the great outdoors.

55
00:04:55,660 --> 00:05:03,360
Any place a shooter could set up some glass plates, bottles, a tin can, or a piece of wood with a bullseye painted on it.

56
00:05:03,361 --> 00:05:10,360
Some of the earlier targets were simple bullseye targets printed on inexpensive paper.

57
00:05:10,361 --> 00:05:16,360
Sometimes there were targets, silhouettes of game animals, birds, rabbits.

58
00:05:16,361 --> 00:05:22,360
Some preferred shooting at playing cards, like legendary Buffalo Bill Cody, who would

59
00:05:22,361 --> 00:05:27,360
show off his shooting skills at exhibitions staged all over the world.

60
00:05:27,361 --> 00:05:33,300
Glass balls filled with feathers were the targets of choice for famed exhibition shooter Annie Oakley.

61
00:05:37,080 --> 00:05:46,340
By the early 1900s, expensive glass balls in China plates were replaced with the return of paper targets made of inexpensive newsprint.

62
00:05:46,341 --> 00:05:54,820
easy to mount and able to take dozens of shots before needing to be thrown away

63
00:05:54,821 --> 00:05:59,360
they were the perfect targets for police departments who needed a cheap way to train

64
00:05:59,361 --> 00:06:06,400
their expanding forces to fight crime in the early 1900s firing ranges were primarily

65
00:06:06,401 --> 00:06:13,040
sticks in the ground where the shooter would put a target on a stick or perhaps a tree

66
00:06:13,041 --> 00:06:18,960
or on a fence post, and then walk a distance back and shoot at it.

67
00:06:19,300 --> 00:06:21,260
Very rudimentary, very unsophisticated.

68
00:06:21,600 --> 00:06:23,700
Military ranges were a little bit more sophisticated

69
00:06:23,701 --> 00:06:26,740
in that generally there would be a post or a camp

70
00:06:26,741 --> 00:06:30,200
where soldiers would train in the use of their firearms,

71
00:06:30,201 --> 00:06:34,780
and they would have a berm or a large mountain or hillside to shoot into,

72
00:06:35,320 --> 00:06:37,460
and then they would line up their targets in a row

73
00:06:37,461 --> 00:06:39,960
and have a known distance from which they would shoot.

74
00:06:39,961 --> 00:06:42,011
During World War I, the U.S.

75
00:06:42,012 --> 00:06:46,920
military experimented with a firing range of a very different kind.

76
00:06:47,880 --> 00:06:49,520
The House of Horror.

77
00:06:50,480 --> 00:06:54,780
It was used to train soldiers in the dangerous art of entering an occupied building.

78
00:06:55,560 --> 00:06:57,940
They would find an abandoned building or warehouse.

79
00:06:58,300 --> 00:07:00,300
Then they would dress up targets with dummies.

80
00:07:02,980 --> 00:07:04,960
Enemy uniforms, friendly uniforms.

81
00:07:04,961 --> 00:07:09,620
They'd pull open doors, have a curtain that the shooter would have to go through.

82
00:07:09,960 --> 00:07:15,960
release a cable and the target would flop out toward the shooter.

83
00:07:15,961 --> 00:07:20,200
Most soldiers, however, sharpened their shooting eye on paper target ranges,

84
00:07:20,201 --> 00:07:24,920
which earned high marks for effectiveness, but low marks for efficiency.

85
00:07:24,921 --> 00:07:27,440
You waited until everyone was through firing.

86
00:07:27,441 --> 00:07:29,960
Before you'd go downrange, hang your targets,

87
00:07:29,961 --> 00:07:35,480
shooters would then go back to the line, do a little bit of shooting, call a ceasefire,

88
00:07:35,481 --> 00:07:39,480
walk back down, change targets.

89
00:07:39,481 --> 00:07:44,240
Some shooters were wounded or even killed by accidental gunfire while retrieving their targets.

90
00:07:45,360 --> 00:07:49,880
But that all changed in the early 1920s, thanks to this man.

91
00:07:50,520 --> 00:07:57,780
Colonel Arthur Caswell was a retired military officer who got involved with the shooting ranges and with law enforcement training.

92
00:07:58,240 --> 00:08:02,380
And one of the first things he did was invent a target retrieval system.

93
00:08:02,520 --> 00:08:06,300
The target was suspended by a cable that was attached to pulleys on both ends.

94
00:08:06,301 --> 00:08:11,300
the shooter would run the pulley system from one end to the other to move the target downrange.

95
00:08:11,640 --> 00:08:15,880
The target was moved up and down with a hand crank that the officer or the shooter

96
00:08:15,881 --> 00:08:18,400
would actually turn himself or herself.

97
00:08:19,640 --> 00:08:25,140
In 1926, Colonel Caswell set up a company to design and build firing ranges.

98
00:08:26,260 --> 00:08:29,300
And now that he had made target retrieval easier and safer,

99
00:08:30,060 --> 00:08:32,800
more and more indoor firing ranges were being built.

100
00:08:32,801 --> 00:08:38,020
But these ranges had to find answers to a potentially deadly problem.

101
00:08:39,360 --> 00:08:41,060
How to stop a speeding bullet.

102
00:08:41,580 --> 00:08:47,460
As ranges became more sophisticated over time, there was introduced the notion of a bullet trap,

103
00:08:48,180 --> 00:08:52,160
or a device by which bullets could be discharged safely indoors,

104
00:08:52,800 --> 00:08:56,560
and then captured or terminated without danger to the shooter.

105
00:08:56,561 --> 00:09:02,440
Drawing inspiration from the centuries-old practice of setting up outdoor firing ranges

106
00:09:02,441 --> 00:09:08,400
in front of hillsides, many early indoor ranges built hills of sand to trap bullets.

107
00:09:08,401 --> 00:09:18,400
A couple of feet deep of fairly coarse sand makes a viable bullet trap.

108
00:09:18,401 --> 00:09:23,320
Viable until thousands of bullets hitting the same spot pile up on each other, creating

109
00:09:23,321 --> 00:09:25,840
dangerous hot spots.

110
00:09:25,841 --> 00:09:31,720
Now you run the risk of bullets hitting that mass and not being absorbed and stopped in the sand,

111
00:09:31,820 --> 00:09:33,940
but bouncing back and creating a hazard on the range.

112
00:09:35,280 --> 00:09:39,120
Then came the idea of combining sand and steel to trap bullets.

113
00:09:40,280 --> 00:09:42,420
It became known as a plate and pit.

114
00:09:43,100 --> 00:09:48,300
Essentially what was used was a piece of steel or armor plate mounted at an angle

115
00:09:48,301 --> 00:09:54,340
so that the bullet would strike the plate and deflect into a pit down below, which was full of sand.

116
00:09:54,341 --> 00:09:57,420
But not all bullets reached the sand.

117
00:09:58,300 --> 00:10:02,940
Sometimes whole bullets or even fragments would ricochet off the steel and strike the shooter.

118
00:10:05,080 --> 00:10:08,080
So the quest continued to build a better bullet trap,

119
00:10:08,660 --> 00:10:12,700
one that would catch the bullet hole and reduce the danger of ricochet.

120
00:10:15,500 --> 00:10:20,440
In the 1980s, Caswell International started making rubber bullet traps.

121
00:10:22,400 --> 00:10:24,000
They tried everything.

122
00:10:24,340 --> 00:10:30,180
Rubber blocks, strips of rubber, and tiny chunks of rubber known as granular rubber bullet traps.

123
00:10:31,700 --> 00:10:37,560
One of the simplest is to take usually shredded or chopped up pieces of used tires

124
00:10:37,561 --> 00:10:42,920
and layer them on some kind of a support structure and layer them a couple of feet thick.

125
00:10:43,120 --> 00:10:47,000
The rubber absorbs the energy of the bullet, slows it down, and contains the bullet.

126
00:10:47,900 --> 00:10:49,880
A great idea, but not perfect.

127
00:10:50,360 --> 00:10:52,620
There was still the risk of dangerous hot spots.

128
00:10:52,621 --> 00:10:57,620
The latest innovation is called a total containment trap.

129
00:10:57,621 --> 00:11:03,620
Those traps use sheets of steel that are mounted at a very, very steep angle.

130
00:11:03,621 --> 00:11:08,620
The idea is, rather than try to stop the bullet, to simply slow it down and redirect it.

131
00:11:08,621 --> 00:11:13,620
The angle is such that the bullet never has a chance to dig into the steel.

132
00:11:13,621 --> 00:11:17,620
It can't get a bite, and the momentum just carries it to the apex of the trap,

133
00:11:17,621 --> 00:11:21,620
and then it's directed into what's called a deceleration chamber,

134
00:11:21,621 --> 00:11:24,120
where the bullet then bounces around in a circle,

135
00:11:24,380 --> 00:11:25,600
kind of beats the bullet up,

136
00:11:25,720 --> 00:11:28,080
and drops it down into a containment basin.

137
00:11:28,860 --> 00:11:31,740
Those traps are probably the best solution

138
00:11:31,741 --> 00:11:33,860
for containing bullets pretty much intact

139
00:11:33,861 --> 00:11:36,060
and keeping stuff from coming back out onto the range.

140
00:11:37,140 --> 00:11:40,920
The total containment trap can handle all types of handguns,

141
00:11:40,921 --> 00:11:44,260
rifles, shotguns, armor-piercing rounds,

142
00:11:44,760 --> 00:11:47,200
even .50-caliber machine gun rounds.

143
00:11:48,900 --> 00:11:50,700
Ready, up!

144
00:11:51,620 --> 00:11:55,940
Bullet traps had gone high-tech, but were targets keeping pace?

145
00:12:00,440 --> 00:12:05,220
A firing range is crucial to learning how to shoot a weapon with deadly accuracy.

146
00:12:08,200 --> 00:12:11,040
But a range is only as good as its targets.

147
00:12:12,060 --> 00:12:16,180
So targets began to change from bullseyes to human forms.

148
00:12:16,420 --> 00:12:19,980
Because law enforcement officers are never threatened by bullseyes,

149
00:12:20,080 --> 00:12:21,520
threats generally come from humans.

150
00:12:21,620 --> 00:12:27,160
So they would try to replicate a silhouette or a form that would be sized appropriately

151
00:12:27,161 --> 00:12:32,180
so that the officer could train in the use of his firearm and directing it around properly.

152
00:12:32,480 --> 00:12:35,980
As time evolved, the training tried to get more and more specific

153
00:12:35,981 --> 00:12:38,700
as to the real problems that would be encountered by the trainees.

154
00:12:40,700 --> 00:12:44,240
What they were encountering was a new breed of criminal,

155
00:12:44,740 --> 00:12:49,320
the machine gun toting gangster of the late 1920s and early 30s.

156
00:12:49,321 --> 00:12:56,000
This is a 100-shot machine gun used by Ma Baca against a federal man in the raid.

157
00:12:57,200 --> 00:13:01,760
So FBI agents armed themselves with rifles, shotguns, and machine guns,

158
00:13:01,960 --> 00:13:04,380
and took to the firing range to learn to shoot them.

159
00:13:05,920 --> 00:13:06,880
Commence firing.

160
00:13:08,440 --> 00:13:10,740
It was one thing to shoot at a fixed target,

161
00:13:11,440 --> 00:13:14,600
but most shootouts with gangsters involved car chases,

162
00:13:15,020 --> 00:13:17,660
which meant their intended targets were in motion.

163
00:13:18,540 --> 00:13:22,100
So FBI agents reasoned that if you're going to hit a moving target,

164
00:13:22,640 --> 00:13:25,080
you should at least practice shooting from a moving car.

165
00:13:26,980 --> 00:13:29,580
Despite the agents' exceptional shooting skills,

166
00:13:29,960 --> 00:13:33,740
innocent people were sometimes getting caught in the crossfire during actual shootouts.

167
00:13:36,400 --> 00:13:40,380
So in an effort to reduce civilian casualties and improve their marksmanship,

168
00:13:41,180 --> 00:13:43,980
the FBI decided to add moving targets to their training.

169
00:13:43,981 --> 00:13:51,980
During the early 1930s, they developed an innovative firing range known as a Hogan's

170
00:13:51,981 --> 00:13:52,980
Alley.

171
00:13:52,981 --> 00:13:59,400
It's a simple plywood facade that represents businesses with doors and windows and with

172
00:13:59,401 --> 00:14:03,100
a system of either levers and pulleys or motors.

173
00:14:03,101 --> 00:14:07,960
The range officer could open a door and expose a target or trip a wire and a target would

174
00:14:07,961 --> 00:14:11,100
swing into an opening.

175
00:14:11,101 --> 00:14:16,740
Most of these targets were photos of innocent people engaged in everyday activities.

176
00:14:16,741 --> 00:14:20,840
But every so often, a photo of a criminal suspect would appear.

177
00:14:20,841 --> 00:14:26,280
And the officer was expected to react appropriately, draw, engage the bad guy, don't hit the good

178
00:14:26,281 --> 00:14:28,860
guy and continue to move through the course.

179
00:14:28,861 --> 00:14:29,860
Gun!

180
00:14:29,861 --> 00:14:30,860
They got guns!

181
00:14:30,861 --> 00:14:32,860
Look out!

182
00:14:32,861 --> 00:14:39,540
Considered extremely innovative, the Hogan's Alley is still used today by some police departments.

183
00:14:39,541 --> 00:14:43,420
The Hogan's Alley is only one type of firing range police officers use.

184
00:14:45,880 --> 00:14:52,700
Other sophisticated range targets have been developed that can also simulate realism and improve an officer's reaction time.

185
00:14:55,540 --> 00:15:02,300
These targets are made of specially treated armor-plated steel, which can stand up to thousands of rounds of bullets.

186
00:15:02,301 --> 00:15:10,580
The sound made by a bullet striking steel lets the shooter know instantly when he has scored a direct hit.

187
00:15:13,660 --> 00:15:16,660
The steel targets come in various shapes and sizes.

188
00:15:18,120 --> 00:15:22,880
From freestanding ones known as pepper poppers, which fall over when struck by a bullet,

189
00:15:23,600 --> 00:15:27,760
to small round targets that are electronically wired to a computer.

190
00:15:27,761 --> 00:15:32,420
They can be programmed to appear from behind an armored steel plate

191
00:15:32,421 --> 00:15:36,600
and then quickly disappear, testing a shooter's reaction time.

192
00:15:37,440 --> 00:15:38,720
Big step! There he is!

193
00:15:38,740 --> 00:15:42,000
You can push the shooters to not just get good accuracy,

194
00:15:42,400 --> 00:15:45,040
but to get good accuracy in a very narrow time frame.

195
00:15:46,020 --> 00:15:48,080
Even paper targets have improved,

196
00:15:48,440 --> 00:15:52,880
from plain black silhouettes to lifelike paintings and photos.

197
00:15:53,940 --> 00:15:57,600
By improving the paper targets and making them look more like,

198
00:15:57,760 --> 00:15:59,900
the challenge that we're actually up against on the street,

199
00:16:00,460 --> 00:16:02,740
it helps not just with the marksmanship training,

200
00:16:02,860 --> 00:16:04,800
but also the mindset we try to develop.

201
00:16:05,860 --> 00:16:07,880
Today, there are targets that move,

202
00:16:08,740 --> 00:16:09,620
targets that turn,

203
00:16:10,540 --> 00:16:13,200
even three-dimensional targets that run.

204
00:16:14,080 --> 00:16:16,840
The controls allow you to start and stop the target,

205
00:16:17,020 --> 00:16:18,140
to move it back and forth,

206
00:16:18,540 --> 00:16:20,340
and use it to demonstrate to our shooters

207
00:16:20,341 --> 00:16:22,980
what it would be like to have someone physically attacking you.

208
00:16:24,720 --> 00:16:26,520
The targets are made of cardboard

209
00:16:26,521 --> 00:16:27,960
with openings for balloons.

210
00:16:29,460 --> 00:16:31,380
A T-shirt slipped over the target

211
00:16:31,381 --> 00:16:33,140
conceals the location of the balloon,

212
00:16:33,860 --> 00:16:35,980
head, chest, or lower torso.

213
00:16:37,640 --> 00:16:39,300
When a bullet strikes the balloon,

214
00:16:39,380 --> 00:16:40,340
it releases a pin,

215
00:16:41,460 --> 00:16:43,640
causing the target to come crashing to the ground.

216
00:16:44,960 --> 00:16:46,140
Police, where are you?

217
00:16:48,740 --> 00:16:51,140
Continually striving for a realistic way

218
00:16:51,141 --> 00:16:52,080
to shoot at targets,

219
00:16:52,540 --> 00:16:54,600
a new type of firing range was developed

220
00:16:54,601 --> 00:16:57,440
that borrowed from the World War I House of Horrors.

221
00:16:58,680 --> 00:17:00,260
It's known as MATCH,

222
00:17:00,820 --> 00:17:03,540
Modular Armored Tactical Combat House.

223
00:17:04,520 --> 00:17:07,120
MATCH is a huge improvement over the earlier shoot houses,

224
00:17:07,620 --> 00:17:09,180
which were called tire houses.

225
00:17:09,920 --> 00:17:11,820
And that's exactly what they were,

226
00:17:12,480 --> 00:17:14,920
a maze of tires stacked eight feet high

227
00:17:14,921 --> 00:17:17,460
to represent the inside of a house or building.

228
00:17:18,520 --> 00:17:20,400
Functional, but far from real.

229
00:17:20,401 --> 00:17:25,400
The match house is made of armor-plated steel.

230
00:17:25,401 --> 00:17:31,400
Over the steel is a layer of plywood with a few inch gap between the two.

231
00:17:31,401 --> 00:17:37,400
When the bullet penetrates the plywood, it hits the steel, the bullet fragments, but

232
00:17:37,401 --> 00:17:42,400
all those fragments are now captured and sandwiched between the plywood and the steel.

233
00:17:42,401 --> 00:17:47,400
We can put furniture inside our shoot house, make it look like a real world environment,

234
00:17:47,401 --> 00:17:49,800
set targets, hang them from the ceiling,

235
00:17:49,900 --> 00:17:52,500
set them on stands in the corner, in the middle of the room.

236
00:17:57,840 --> 00:18:00,740
Some police departments have taken the concept of shoot houses

237
00:18:00,840 --> 00:18:04,760
a step further and turned themselves into live targets.

238
00:18:09,860 --> 00:18:13,300
Inside this shoot house, cops are firing bullets at each other

239
00:18:13,400 --> 00:18:16,000
in the ultimate game of good guys versus bad guys.

240
00:18:17,400 --> 00:18:20,880
This is known as force-on-force training.

241
00:18:22,580 --> 00:18:24,860
Force-on-force training is very helpful,

242
00:18:25,120 --> 00:18:28,600
where everybody puts on the padded suits and the protective gear

243
00:18:28,601 --> 00:18:30,180
and shoots at each other.

244
00:18:31,100 --> 00:18:33,780
The ammunition they're using looks like a real bullet,

245
00:18:34,220 --> 00:18:37,780
but it is actually specially manufactured simulated rounds

246
00:18:37,781 --> 00:18:39,640
filled with salt or paint.

247
00:18:40,660 --> 00:18:44,140
It's great to have options to include in our training

248
00:18:44,141 --> 00:18:50,840
because the goal, of course, is to teach people that they can prevail

249
00:18:50,841 --> 00:18:54,600
in the environment that we expect them to go into.

250
00:18:54,820 --> 00:18:55,520
Good, excellent!

251
00:18:55,820 --> 00:19:00,280
Terrorist attacks have served to put more law enforcement departments on high alert.

252
00:19:00,281 --> 00:19:05,240
We expect that there will be a higher demand for training facilities

253
00:19:05,241 --> 00:19:07,440
for those people carrying firearms.

254
00:19:08,880 --> 00:19:10,960
Facilities like Thunder Ranch,

255
00:19:10,961 --> 00:19:14,940
which offers a wide variety of real-life environments and challenges.

256
00:19:16,040 --> 00:19:17,600
Stop! Drop the weapon!

257
00:19:18,960 --> 00:19:24,060
Thunder Ranch occupies 2,400 acres outside of San Antonio, Texas.

258
00:19:25,240 --> 00:19:30,700
It features firing range attractions with names like the Terminator and the Tower,

259
00:19:31,400 --> 00:19:33,640
both of which are elaborate indoor shoot houses.

260
00:19:35,220 --> 00:19:36,660
Stop! Drop the weapon!

261
00:19:36,661 --> 00:19:40,140
And there's the charger deck.

262
00:19:40,240 --> 00:19:42,940
Which is a contraption that has moving targets

263
00:19:42,941 --> 00:19:47,000
that can all be operated independently to attack the shooter.

264
00:19:48,600 --> 00:19:51,700
Their greatest attraction of all is Thunderville,

265
00:19:51,980 --> 00:19:54,900
one of the most spectacular Hogan's alleys in the world.

266
00:19:58,420 --> 00:20:02,840
Thunderville is 200 feet long and features over 60 targets.

267
00:20:02,841 --> 00:20:09,240
It's just a very, very innovative, very well thought out training environment where you

268
00:20:09,241 --> 00:20:21,560
can learn a number of very worthwhile skills.

269
00:20:21,561 --> 00:20:24,280
This is target practice military style.

270
00:20:32,840 --> 00:20:41,980
It's the crowning achievement in tank firing ranges,

271
00:20:42,260 --> 00:20:44,480
and it's located at Fort Irwin, California.

272
00:20:45,620 --> 00:20:50,800
The essence of most training is acquiring a skill,

273
00:20:51,360 --> 00:20:54,060
and to keeping that skill, you have to practice.

274
00:20:55,060 --> 00:20:58,080
And the U.S. military has been doing a lot of practice

275
00:20:58,081 --> 00:21:00,560
in a lot of places during the past hundred years.

276
00:21:02,840 --> 00:21:09,720
But few weapons in the military arsenal have inspired more creativity in the design of its firing ranges than the tank,

277
00:21:10,560 --> 00:21:12,760
the workhorse of the U.S. military.

278
00:21:14,400 --> 00:21:20,820
The tank has been around since World War I, but it really made its presence felt on the battlefields of World War II.

279
00:21:21,680 --> 00:21:26,080
During World War II, we began to get very interested in tanks.

280
00:21:26,081 --> 00:21:34,320
We saw what the Germans did in 1939 with the Blitzkrieg.

281
00:21:34,660 --> 00:21:36,820
We realized that tanks were important.

282
00:21:39,380 --> 00:21:43,540
And with that realization came the advent of tank firing ranges.

283
00:21:45,380 --> 00:21:50,320
One of the earliest dates from around 1940 at Fort Knox, Kentucky.

284
00:21:51,460 --> 00:21:52,220
The target?

285
00:21:52,221 --> 00:21:56,060
old cars.

286
00:21:56,960 --> 00:22:00,260
Let's face it, that's what Americans are great at producing,

287
00:22:00,600 --> 00:22:02,920
old, rusted, worn-out car bodies.

288
00:22:05,260 --> 00:22:08,280
They make very convenient and useful targets.

289
00:22:09,660 --> 00:22:13,640
A typical tank target range was about 2,000 meters wide

290
00:22:13,641 --> 00:22:15,280
and 5,000 meters deep.

291
00:22:16,340 --> 00:22:18,720
Why the extra 3,000 meters in depth?

292
00:22:18,721 --> 00:22:21,720
The other 3,000 meters would have been a buffer zone

293
00:22:21,721 --> 00:22:27,620
so that if they engaged a target at 2,000 meters and they shot over,

294
00:22:28,200 --> 00:22:31,100
it wouldn't hit civilians or something else.

295
00:22:31,240 --> 00:22:37,180
And that's pretty close to where the size of a lot of ranges that are still used today.

296
00:22:38,320 --> 00:22:40,760
Early tank ranges were not without flaws.

297
00:22:41,400 --> 00:22:47,000
The big problem with the old tank ranges, where you just put car bodies out there or panels,

298
00:22:47,001 --> 00:22:52,000
is they're stationary, they're just sitting there, and it's not realistic.

299
00:22:52,160 --> 00:22:56,460
That's not the way you would use that tank to engage targets in combat.

300
00:22:58,000 --> 00:23:02,640
So Caswell International came up with the idea of building a plywood tank target.

301
00:23:03,020 --> 00:23:09,080
You cut out a silhouette that either looks like the front of a tank or the side of a tank.

302
00:23:09,500 --> 00:23:13,080
Obviously, you don't want to put too many of the side views out there

303
00:23:13,081 --> 00:23:17,560
because that doesn't test your gunnery as well as the front view does.

304
00:23:17,960 --> 00:23:20,940
If it looks like the front of a tank, you've got a smaller target.

305
00:23:23,100 --> 00:23:28,720
The side view target is 20 feet long, 8 feet high, and weighs almost 400 pounds.

306
00:23:29,780 --> 00:23:32,460
The front view target is about half that size.

307
00:23:33,980 --> 00:23:38,740
Since most tank target practice involves the use of dummy rounds rather than live ammunition,

308
00:23:39,440 --> 00:23:41,180
the target can last a long time.

309
00:23:41,181 --> 00:23:45,089
The largest round that's in use today in the U.S.

310
00:23:45,090 --> 00:23:48,600
military is a 120 millimeter diameter round.

311
00:23:48,601 --> 00:23:53,900
The targets can withstand maybe 50 or 60 of these rounds before they have to be replaced.

312
00:23:55,820 --> 00:24:00,940
Like law enforcement, the military is constantly striving for realism on its firing ranges.

313
00:24:02,400 --> 00:24:08,540
So during the 1950s, Caswell designed a tank target that moves back and forth on railroad tracks,

314
00:24:08,541 --> 00:24:11,640
which are laid down behind protective earthworms.

315
00:24:12,640 --> 00:24:16,000
It's known as MAT, Moving Armor Target.

316
00:24:16,620 --> 00:24:21,020
Today's Caswell Moving Armor Target system is really an electric car.

317
00:24:21,220 --> 00:24:26,380
We think we even beat the auto industry in terms of coming up with a functional electric car.

318
00:24:26,700 --> 00:24:29,040
And we also provide it with its own power source.

319
00:24:29,300 --> 00:24:31,760
The mat has got batteries and a generator.

320
00:24:31,980 --> 00:24:36,000
As long as you keep fuel in the generator, it'll run continuously for days on end.

321
00:24:36,001 --> 00:24:42,180
Even though blank rounds of ammunition are used, tank target practice still looks and

322
00:24:42,181 --> 00:24:44,125
sounds like a real battlefield.

323
00:24:44,126 --> 00:24:47,700
That's because MAT has been equipped with special effects

324
00:24:47,701 --> 00:24:50,120
that simulate explosions and gunfire.

325
00:24:50,121 --> 00:24:57,800
The special effects on our targets are all designed to put on a show for the trainees

326
00:24:57,801 --> 00:25:01,060
and make them feel like they're in real combat.

327
00:25:01,061 --> 00:25:06,300
In the 1960s, Caswell introduced pop-up targets on the tank firing range.

328
00:25:06,301 --> 00:25:10,540
The target is presented to the shooter as the target carrier runs along the railroad

329
00:25:10,541 --> 00:25:16,480
tracks protected by a berm so that when the tank or the armored vehicle is looking at

330
00:25:16,481 --> 00:25:19,580
the target, all they really see is the silhouette itself.

331
00:25:19,581 --> 00:25:24,420
When the target is struck by a round, then it conceals, indicating to the shooter that

332
00:25:24,421 --> 00:25:25,780
he has hit the target.

333
00:25:25,781 --> 00:25:31,040
This is a much more realistic way of training because that's the way it happens in combat.

334
00:25:31,041 --> 00:25:33,400
All of a sudden, you see a tank.

335
00:25:33,540 --> 00:25:38,780
You can have two tanks pop up at once, one near and one far,

336
00:25:38,900 --> 00:25:43,460
and see if the crew is smart enough to engage that near target first,

337
00:25:43,540 --> 00:25:46,340
because that's the one that's most likely to get you.

338
00:25:47,700 --> 00:25:50,900
Engaging one or two tanks can test any gunner's skills.

339
00:25:52,060 --> 00:25:53,880
Imagine trying to take on 1,000.

340
00:25:55,180 --> 00:26:00,060
That's how many pop-up tank targets are on the firing range at Fort Irwin, California.

341
00:26:01,040 --> 00:26:12,079
There are no moving targets on that range because it would have been too difficult to put a moving target out there and then have the tank battalion go past it

342
00:26:12,080 --> 00:26:14,440
because, remember, it's on a rail.

343
00:26:15,160 --> 00:26:16,720
And so they would have torn up the rail.

344
00:26:16,721 --> 00:26:22,360
Instead, they simulate motion by having a computer scenario

345
00:26:22,361 --> 00:26:26,960
that brings targets up in a planned way

346
00:26:26,961 --> 00:26:31,460
so that they can simulate either attacking you or going away from you.

347
00:26:31,660 --> 00:26:34,740
And with a thousand of them, they can simulate that pretty well.

348
00:26:36,260 --> 00:26:40,740
The U.S. military has a fleet of more than 7,600 tanks.

349
00:26:41,240 --> 00:26:44,360
But tanks are only one weapon of mass destruction in its arsenal.

350
00:26:44,361 --> 00:26:49,400
A suspicious plane streaks across the California skies.

351
00:26:50,280 --> 00:26:53,620
A U.S. fighter jet picks up the enemy plane over the Mojave Desert.

352
00:26:54,380 --> 00:26:59,040
The top gun pilot locks in on its target and fires a deadly Sidewinder missile.

353
00:27:01,180 --> 00:27:04,500
Fortunately, the enemy in this case was just an old U.S. fighter jet

354
00:27:04,501 --> 00:27:07,400
that was remote-controlled from a command post miles away.

355
00:27:08,360 --> 00:27:13,460
If the test is successful and goes correctly and the missile behaves as you expect it to behave,

356
00:27:13,461 --> 00:27:17,000
you have a very high likelihood for several thousand pounds of F-4

357
00:27:17,001 --> 00:27:19,200
to come down and crash a ball of fire.

358
00:27:20,760 --> 00:27:23,360
Targets going down in flames is a regular sight

359
00:27:23,361 --> 00:27:26,700
at the U.S. military's favorite large arms firing range,

360
00:27:27,880 --> 00:27:29,140
China Lake, California.

361
00:27:31,420 --> 00:27:35,600
Founded in the 1940s and covering more than 1.1 million acres

362
00:27:35,601 --> 00:27:36,580
in the Mojave Desert,

363
00:27:37,120 --> 00:27:39,900
it's the largest firing range of its kind in the world.

364
00:27:39,901 --> 00:27:43,980
It's a pretty busy place.

365
00:27:44,080 --> 00:27:47,520
We run somewhere in the order of a thousand test missions a year.

366
00:27:48,580 --> 00:27:53,220
We have roughly 300 people that work in this department doing testing day in, day out.

367
00:27:53,720 --> 00:27:57,300
A small test may have only a half a dozen of those people actually doing something.

368
00:27:57,580 --> 00:28:00,300
A larger test will have a couple hundred people involved.

369
00:28:03,660 --> 00:28:07,540
So what type of target practice is going on at this top-secret facility?

370
00:28:07,541 --> 00:28:11,760
Everything from Sidewinder air-to-air missiles

371
00:28:11,761 --> 00:28:15,360
to Tomahawk Sea-to-land cruise missiles

372
00:28:15,361 --> 00:28:19,360
to harm and slam air-to-surface missiles

373
00:28:19,361 --> 00:28:26,680
and dozens of other weapons.

374
00:28:27,100 --> 00:28:29,120
We're not just coming in and throwing lots of bombs

375
00:28:29,121 --> 00:28:32,060
and firing lots of missiles and having lots of high explosives.

376
00:28:32,240 --> 00:28:35,140
The tests that we're doing are very, very closely scripted

377
00:28:35,141 --> 00:28:37,360
with very specific goals and objectives.

378
00:28:37,540 --> 00:28:44,740
That's comforting for the people of Southern California, since the China Lake firing range

379
00:28:44,741 --> 00:28:50,080
is located just 150 miles from Los Angeles.

380
00:28:50,081 --> 00:28:55,200
The military not only owns the 1.1 million acres of desert, but it also controls the

381
00:28:55,201 --> 00:28:59,500
airspace from the ground up.

382
00:28:59,501 --> 00:29:05,040
It has its own air traffic controllers, monitoring the more than 200 military and commercial flights

383
00:29:05,041 --> 00:29:07,340
that pass near China Lake on any given day.

384
00:29:08,780 --> 00:29:12,300
Their primary duty is to keep our internal restricted areas

385
00:29:12,301 --> 00:29:14,680
clear of non-participating aircraft

386
00:29:14,681 --> 00:29:16,520
to make sure that if we're firing a missile,

387
00:29:16,840 --> 00:29:19,240
it's going to go at the intended target that we want it to go to.

388
00:29:20,400 --> 00:29:22,920
A good idea, since some of the target practice

389
00:29:22,921 --> 00:29:24,880
involves cruise and tomahawk missiles

390
00:29:24,881 --> 00:29:26,900
launched from warships and submarines

391
00:29:26,901 --> 00:29:28,920
stationed off the coast of California.

392
00:29:31,480 --> 00:29:34,740
The missiles are aimed at targets on the China Lake range.

393
00:29:35,040 --> 00:29:39,160
but their 200-mile flight path takes them over heavily populated areas.

394
00:29:40,580 --> 00:29:43,560
These missiles are capable of leveling small towns,

395
00:29:44,060 --> 00:29:46,500
which is why the test team takes extreme measures

396
00:29:46,501 --> 00:29:48,860
to ensure that disasters never happen.

397
00:29:49,220 --> 00:29:54,800
The missile is entirely chased with a chase aircraft through its entire route.

398
00:29:55,220 --> 00:29:58,940
They have the opportunity, if they need to, to actually take control and fly the missile,

399
00:29:59,240 --> 00:30:02,740
or if need be, they can bring the missile down and use the flight termination on the system.

400
00:30:02,741 --> 00:30:07,980
The missiles fly about a thousand feet off the ground and are constantly monitored by

401
00:30:07,981 --> 00:30:11,900
scientists and engineers from this command control center.

402
00:30:11,901 --> 00:30:19,180
They, too, have secret ways of safely destroying a missile if it strays off course.

403
00:30:19,181 --> 00:30:25,940
Fortunately, the majority of missiles never deviate from their flight path, and the few

404
00:30:25,941 --> 00:30:31,260
that have were destroyed without anyone being hurt.

405
00:30:31,261 --> 00:30:34,360
A lot of thought goes into the type of target used at China Lake.

406
00:30:34,880 --> 00:30:37,420
It's often influenced by the weapon being tested.

407
00:30:38,640 --> 00:30:42,460
We could have targets that could be built in a day by welding a couple C-vans together,

408
00:30:42,820 --> 00:30:46,340
or we have targets that might take three or four months of construction to work and build

409
00:30:46,341 --> 00:30:48,960
to beat the specific requirements of that test.

410
00:30:49,260 --> 00:30:53,220
We've also done a lot of work with anti-tank systems such as the Maverick.

411
00:30:54,160 --> 00:30:58,680
A Maverick missile homes in on its target by detecting heat radiating from it.

412
00:30:58,681 --> 00:31:00,340
They'll bring in tank hulks.

413
00:31:00,341 --> 00:31:01,340
They no longer have an engine.

414
00:31:01,341 --> 00:31:03,920
They'll actually use charcoal inside the heat,

415
00:31:03,921 --> 00:31:06,380
the engine compartment to heat them.

416
00:31:06,381 --> 00:31:11,020
The Maverick detects the heat and moves in for the kill.

417
00:31:11,021 --> 00:31:14,420
Missiles are also routinely fired at moving targets.

418
00:31:14,421 --> 00:31:16,240
We do have a limited number of tanks

419
00:31:16,241 --> 00:31:20,600
that are actually remote controlled from several miles away.

420
00:31:20,601 --> 00:31:23,480
Dune buggies also make great moving targets.

421
00:31:23,481 --> 00:31:26,100
Their speed can be varied to match that of any tank

422
00:31:26,101 --> 00:31:28,680
or armored vehicle.

423
00:31:28,681 --> 00:31:31,240
And people, that's what they do for their job,

424
00:31:31,340 --> 00:31:33,220
create targets to meet the customer's needs.

425
00:31:34,000 --> 00:31:35,860
And when the customer is Uncle Sam,

426
00:31:36,540 --> 00:31:40,280
targets from bridges to bunkers have been built and blown up.

427
00:31:45,220 --> 00:31:47,420
Bombs and missiles are crucial in war,

428
00:31:47,940 --> 00:31:51,340
but traditional wars are usually won or lost on the ground

429
00:31:51,341 --> 00:31:55,120
by soldiers armed with rifles and machine guns.

430
00:31:55,121 --> 00:32:02,120
Military firing ranges have come a long way from their earliest days of paper bullseyes on sticks.

431
00:32:02,121 --> 00:32:07,120
Just ask any soldier who got stuck with the assignment of retrieving targets.

432
00:32:07,121 --> 00:32:10,120
These people are actually known as butt pullers.

433
00:32:10,121 --> 00:32:14,120
The targets were presented by raising them from what was known as a target pit.

434
00:32:14,121 --> 00:32:20,120
And at the bottom of the pit there would actually be humans stationed at each target location

435
00:32:20,121 --> 00:32:24,580
location, who would then mount the target on a device that would elevate it vertically

436
00:32:24,581 --> 00:32:26,381
and present the target to the shooter.

437
00:32:26,382 --> 00:32:28,940
Once the firing was completed, they'd lower the target

438
00:32:28,941 --> 00:32:31,088
and record their score.

439
00:32:31,089 --> 00:32:37,160
Military ranges remained virtually unchanged until the mid-1950s,

440
00:32:37,740 --> 00:32:42,920
when a surprising study conducted after the Korean War exposed problems in the way soldiers

441
00:32:42,921 --> 00:32:43,700
were being trained.

442
00:32:44,800 --> 00:32:47,820
In many instances in the Korean War,

443
00:32:48,500 --> 00:32:52,020
the overwhelming majority of a platoon or a company

444
00:32:52,021 --> 00:32:55,500
who were engaged in a firefight would not shoot.

445
00:32:56,200 --> 00:32:58,220
Only a small number would shoot.

446
00:32:59,020 --> 00:33:02,440
Caswell International, the same company that had created advances

447
00:33:02,441 --> 00:33:05,100
in police target ranges and tank target ranges,

448
00:33:05,760 --> 00:33:08,040
revolutionized the military rifle range

449
00:33:08,041 --> 00:33:11,080
with the creation of an infantry pop-up target.

450
00:33:12,920 --> 00:33:18,220
It will pop up and the soldier will have a certain amount of time to engage it,

451
00:33:18,640 --> 00:33:20,700
and if he doesn't engage it, it goes back down.

452
00:33:20,860 --> 00:33:25,740
Or if he hits it, then it goes back down and he knows he hit the target.

453
00:33:26,760 --> 00:33:29,960
The targets are hardwired with sensors and cables

454
00:33:29,961 --> 00:33:32,460
attached to a control room on the firing range.

455
00:33:33,660 --> 00:33:36,920
A trainer can program various ways to train his troops,

456
00:33:36,921 --> 00:33:41,660
turning these plastic infantrymen into a new generation of smart targets.

457
00:33:42,920 --> 00:33:46,920
In real battles, soldiers don't just move up and down.

458
00:33:46,921 --> 00:33:49,920
They also move side to side.

459
00:33:49,921 --> 00:33:51,920
So borrowing from the design of their tank target,

460
00:33:51,921 --> 00:33:54,920
CASWELL incorporated the use of railroad tracks

461
00:33:54,921 --> 00:33:57,920
to create the moving infantry target,

462
00:33:57,921 --> 00:34:01,920
which could roll back and forth as well as pop up.

463
00:34:01,921 --> 00:34:03,920
We started out just pulling it back and forth

464
00:34:03,921 --> 00:34:07,920
at a specific speed, and now we've evolved up to a fully self-contained unit

465
00:34:07,921 --> 00:34:10,920
that will go back and forth on the tracks,

466
00:34:10,921 --> 00:34:16,920
fire all the simulators or return fire that you might expect from an enemy soldier or a perpetrator.

467
00:34:18,920 --> 00:34:23,920
Caswell has even designed a three-dimensional moving target, which looks like a real person.

468
00:34:25,920 --> 00:34:30,920
Shooting at targets, even ones that look like humans, is great for sharpening a soldier's skills.

469
00:34:33,920 --> 00:34:37,920
But the best way to prepare for battle is to fire at live targets.

470
00:34:40,920 --> 00:34:46,540
Lasers have made it possible.

471
00:34:48,160 --> 00:34:51,520
The military uses a system called MILES,

472
00:34:52,480 --> 00:34:55,140
Multiple Integrated Laser Engagement System.

473
00:34:56,900 --> 00:35:00,860
Receptors are placed on a vest or helmet worn by soldiers.

474
00:35:02,100 --> 00:35:04,980
The soldiers are then armed with weapons that fire lasers.

475
00:35:04,981 --> 00:35:10,660
You can then engage those targets just as though you were shooting live fire at them,

476
00:35:10,661 --> 00:35:14,700
and see who is actually hitting someone and killing them.

477
00:35:15,340 --> 00:35:18,560
When the laser strikes the sensor, it scores a hit.

478
00:35:19,160 --> 00:35:24,440
That is the most realistic force-on-force training anybody's ever had.

479
00:35:27,240 --> 00:35:30,280
The atomic bomb brought an end to World War II,

480
00:35:31,140 --> 00:35:34,960
at the same time ushering in an era of nuclear testing.

481
00:35:36,860 --> 00:35:39,160
As comedian Bob Hope once said,

482
00:35:39,161 --> 00:35:47,060
As soon as the war ended, we located the one spot on Earth that hadn't been touched by the war, and blew it to hell.

483
00:35:49,080 --> 00:35:54,460
That place was Bikini Atoll, a tiny speck of land in the South Pacific.

484
00:35:55,480 --> 00:35:59,060
It would become the world's most famous military firing range.

485
00:35:59,780 --> 00:36:06,360
It was thought that after World War II, there ought to be a test, a Navy test, of atomic bombs,

486
00:36:06,361 --> 00:36:10,620
and particularly to see how atomic bombs affected ships,

487
00:36:10,720 --> 00:36:12,600
you know, would it sink them, destroy them, vaporize them.

488
00:36:13,560 --> 00:36:15,300
Because of its remote location,

489
00:36:15,820 --> 00:36:17,880
Bikini Atoll was chosen for that test.

490
00:36:18,860 --> 00:36:23,140
In March 1946, the 167 Bikinians

491
00:36:23,141 --> 00:36:25,260
who called this tropical paradise home

492
00:36:25,261 --> 00:36:27,740
were rounded up by the U.S. Navy

493
00:36:27,741 --> 00:36:29,780
and moved to another island.

494
00:36:30,980 --> 00:36:32,520
Then a fleet of ships,

495
00:36:32,521 --> 00:36:34,780
from aircraft carriers and battleships

496
00:36:34,781 --> 00:36:38,720
to destroyers and tugs were anchored in the nearby lagoon.

497
00:36:39,160 --> 00:36:43,220
The United States actually constructs what becomes the world's fourth largest navy in terms of number of ships.

498
00:36:43,440 --> 00:36:45,980
Well over 150 ships are put in the lagoon.

499
00:36:46,860 --> 00:36:51,660
In the center was the battleship Nevada, the bullseye for the atomic bomb.

500
00:36:53,140 --> 00:36:56,100
The project was called Operation Crossroads.

501
00:36:56,101 --> 00:37:06,100
And in June of 1946, the same type of bomb used to level Nagasaki was loaded onto a B-29 and dropped onto its awaiting targets.

502
00:37:06,101 --> 00:37:07,100
targets.

503
00:37:26,720 --> 00:37:29,300
The nuclear arms race had officially begun.

504
00:37:29,301 --> 00:37:33,660
Amazingly, the blast sank only 13 of the 150 ships.

505
00:37:37,780 --> 00:37:43,760
In 1952, nearby Eniwetok Atoll was the site of the first hydrogen bomb test.

506
00:37:46,720 --> 00:37:50,900
As Cold War tensions heated up between the United States and the Soviet Union,

507
00:37:51,360 --> 00:37:52,060
the U.S.

508
00:37:52,061 --> 00:37:57,160
military wanted a place more private than the Pacific Ocean to carry out their nuclear tests.

509
00:37:57,161 --> 00:38:02,160
That place was the Yucca Flats in the Nevada desert.

510
00:38:02,161 --> 00:38:05,160
The first set of tests going out there are called Operation Ranger.

511
00:38:05,161 --> 00:38:10,160
The planes are loaded with atomic bombs at one of the air bases nearby in Nevada,

512
00:38:10,161 --> 00:38:13,160
flown up to the test site and dropped on a stationary target.

513
00:38:13,161 --> 00:38:20,160
In some cases, the targets were entire towns, specially constructed for the tests,

514
00:38:20,161 --> 00:38:23,160
to measure the destructive force of a nuclear warhead.

515
00:38:23,161 --> 00:38:28,040
For a long time, all post-war testing was above ground.

516
00:38:28,041 --> 00:38:29,040
Bomb away.

517
00:38:31,560 --> 00:38:34,580
Approximately 200 tests were carried out above ground,

518
00:38:35,060 --> 00:38:38,900
but radiation fallout led to health and environmental concerns,

519
00:38:39,680 --> 00:38:46,780
forcing nuclear tests to go underground in the early 1960s.

520
00:38:46,781 --> 00:38:54,820
One of the challenges, major challenges you face though, is that you're not drilling a

521
00:38:54,821 --> 00:38:57,640
little hole, you're drilling a big hole.

522
00:38:57,641 --> 00:39:01,900
Big, as in thousands of feet deep.

523
00:39:01,901 --> 00:39:06,680
Unlike above ground tests where bombs streak towards Earth at hundreds of miles an hour,

524
00:39:06,681 --> 00:39:12,000
an underground test requires that a bomb gently reach its target.

525
00:39:12,001 --> 00:39:16,940
How do you drop 100,000 pounds down a hole that's thousands of feet deep, not drop it,

526
00:39:16,941 --> 00:39:22,840
not break it, not bend it, not shake it, and position it right where you want it to be

527
00:39:22,841 --> 00:39:25,240
positioned down hole?

528
00:39:25,241 --> 00:39:30,200
Specially designed, one-of-a-kind cranes were built to handle the job.

529
00:39:30,201 --> 00:39:36,240
Cranes that can lift 100,000 pounds and drop it inch by inch very slowly down, very gently

530
00:39:36,241 --> 00:39:40,540
down this hole, position it, hold it.

531
00:39:40,541 --> 00:39:45,080
Once in place, the bomb, which was wired with cables and electronic devices for measuring

532
00:39:45,081 --> 00:39:51,700
the impact of the blast, was buried under thousands of tons of rock, gravel and cement

533
00:39:51,701 --> 00:39:54,780
and was finally ready for detonation.

534
00:39:54,781 --> 00:40:01,460
When a nuclear device explodes underground, it vaporizes the rock and everything around

535
00:40:01,461 --> 00:40:06,860
it creates a huge cavity underground, but it kind of looks like a moonscape.

536
00:40:06,861 --> 00:40:10,100
The Nevada desert is dotted with dozens of these moon-like craters.

537
00:40:11,360 --> 00:40:15,900
Scars of an era that ended with America's last underground test in 1992.

538
00:40:19,380 --> 00:40:23,240
The military claims these craters pose no threat of radiation.

539
00:40:27,980 --> 00:40:31,940
Taking target practice at a military firing range can be very costly.

540
00:40:32,940 --> 00:40:36,400
But a new generation of firing ranges is changing that.

541
00:40:36,860 --> 00:40:42,860
They are inexpensive to operate, portable, and can fit in rooms smaller than a basketball court.

542
00:40:44,860 --> 00:40:50,860
Like their counterparts in law enforcement, the military has turned to virtual firing ranges for training,

543
00:40:50,861 --> 00:40:57,860
using weapons modified with lasers, many of them again provided by FATS.

544
00:40:57,861 --> 00:41:03,860
From pistols through light and heavy machine guns, anti-tank weapons, air defense weapons,

545
00:41:03,861 --> 00:41:07,700
weapons, armor and anti-armor weapons.

546
00:41:07,701 --> 00:41:10,540
Soldiers can be placed in almost any military conflict

547
00:41:10,541 --> 00:41:12,680
imaginable.

548
00:41:12,681 --> 00:41:15,000
At the flip of a few computer keys,

549
00:41:15,001 --> 00:41:19,400
a trainee can be taking on an armored tank brigade

550
00:41:19,401 --> 00:41:22,100
or hunting down guerrilla forces.

551
00:41:22,101 --> 00:41:23,800
The simulators to support Rangers

552
00:41:23,801 --> 00:41:26,360
are getting smarter every day.

553
00:41:26,361 --> 00:41:27,980
So smart, in fact, that soldiers

554
00:41:27,981 --> 00:41:30,820
in different parts of the world can all be linked together

555
00:41:30,821 --> 00:41:34,780
to fire at the same virtual enemy.

556
00:41:34,781 --> 00:41:37,200
And the results of one squad can be seen

557
00:41:37,201 --> 00:41:40,560
on the screen of another squad.

558
00:41:40,561 --> 00:41:42,640
There's even a new generation of simulators

559
00:41:42,641 --> 00:41:46,380
for taking target practice from inside moving vehicles,

560
00:41:46,381 --> 00:41:48,000
like boats, jeeps, and tanks.

561
00:41:50,960 --> 00:41:53,140
What might tomorrow hold?

562
00:41:53,141 --> 00:41:55,720
More realism and more interactivity

563
00:41:55,721 --> 00:41:57,860
between the shooter and the target.

564
00:41:57,861 --> 00:42:00,820
Holographs, maybe an answer to that in the future.

565
00:42:00,821 --> 00:42:06,180
But will holograms and laser simulators ever totally replace live ammunition firing ranges?

566
00:42:06,880 --> 00:42:11,460
You can't do all of your training inside of a room.

567
00:42:11,960 --> 00:42:13,540
People in law enforcement agree.

568
00:42:14,400 --> 00:42:18,420
The video interactive simulators teach us a lot of good stuff.

569
00:42:19,300 --> 00:42:20,080
They're not real bullets.

570
00:42:20,081 --> 00:42:24,280
The Army will always have live fire ranges, but at some point,

571
00:42:24,800 --> 00:42:28,340
we're always going to get that tank or that artillery piece

572
00:42:28,341 --> 00:42:32,620
or that rifle out there on a range and see how it does

573
00:42:32,621 --> 00:42:36,700
and how the crew does or the soldier does with real ammunition.

574
00:42:37,600 --> 00:42:39,380
That'll never go away.

575
00:43:57,120 --> 00:43:58,320
The Appoinette

